&lt;p&gt;Consider $N$ as a positive integer which is divided into $k$ identical segments, such that $r = N/k$, hence $N = r + r + \cdots + r$.&lt;br&gt; The product of these segments is denoted as $P$, where $P = r 	imes r 	imes \cdots 	imes r = r^k$.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For instance, when $11$ is divided into five identical segments, $11 = 2.2 + 2.2 + 2.2 + 2.2 + 2.2$, and thus $P = 2.2^5 = 51.53632$.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Let $M(N) = P_{\mathrm{max}}$ for a specific value of $N$.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Interestingly, the maximum for $N = 11$ is obtained by dividing eleven into four identical segments which results in $P_{\mathrm{max}} = (11/4)^4$; hence, $M(11) = 14641/256 = 57.19140625$, which is a terminating decimal.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Contrarily, for $N = 8$ the maximum is reached by dividing it into three identical segments, thus $M(8) = 512/27$, which is a non-terminating decimal.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Let $D(N) = N$ if $M(N)$ is a non-terminating decimal and $D(N) = -N$ if $M(N)$ is a terminating decimal.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;For instance, $\sum D(N)$ for $5 \le N \le 100$ equals $2438$.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Determine the value of $\sum D(N)$ for $5 \le N \le 10000$.&lt;/p&gt;
